   ],
   "duration": 40.467,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Rust Systems Engineering starts here.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 2.269
    },
    {
     "text": "You've read the Book through chapter ten. Now ownership becomes a design tool... starting with its smallest unit.",
     "start": 2.689,
     "end": 8.556
    },
    {
     "text": "One value. One owner. One destruction.",
     "start": 8.976,
     "end": 12.904
    },
    {
     "text": "Picture an ingest service that ships batches of one million bytes.",
     "start": 13.724,
     "end": 17.354
    },
    {
     "text": "Calling ship on a batch was rejected by the compiler... the use-after-move error.",
     "start": 17.774,
     "end": 22.773
    },
    {
     "text": "The review took the compiler's own suggestion. Clone.",
     "start": 23.193,
     "end": 26.753
    },
    {
     "text": "It works. But now every single batch pays one extra allocation, and a one million byte copy.",
     "start": 27.573,
     "end": 34.062
    },
    {
     "text": "By the end, you'll know why Rust refused... and fix it without copying a single byte.",
     "start": 34.482,
     "end": 40.117
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.5,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 142,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "02": {
   "hash": "21ac5caf96c1f72d",
   "takes": [
    "93bf8f4f26b62f78dda9",
    "de63d67d3a196810171b",
    "7501f42f057106825c04"
   ],
   "duration": 16.656,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Let's start with the smallest case.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 2.429
    },
    {
     "text": "A string is a small header on the stack... a pointer, a length, and a capacity... pointing at text on the heap.",
     "start": 2.849,
     "end": 9.736
    },
    {
     "text": "So when you write let s two equals s one... what exactly gets copied?",
     "start": 10.556,
     "end": 16.306
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.4,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 151,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "02a": {
   "hash": "ff3d209fbd79df5c",
   "takes": [
    "f75a7961f87953040dcf",
    "8f981c5511a558275307"
   ],
   "duration": 11.198,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Just the header. Pointer, length and capacity. Twenty-four bytes.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 5.663
    },
    {
     "text": "The text itself never moves... and Rust now treats s one as gone.",
     "start": 6.083,
     "end": 10.848
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.2,
   "true_peak": -1.8,
   "wpm": 123,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "03": {
   "hash": "abcd40a036df0d8b",
   "takes": [
    "05deec877ec490a65f92",
    "e17fee5572af9705679b",
    "9ad29e7c31990effec7b",
    "196edef4cef2459a6857",
    "37007bc4387aa2bf5b8c"
   ],
   "duration": 25.525,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Here's why s one has to die.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 2.358
    },
    {
     "text": "Imagine both bindings still owned the buffer.",
     "start": 2.778,
     "end": 5.09
    },
    {
     "text": "At the closing brace, both would free it. A double free... the classic memory bug that can corrupt the allocator.",
     "start": 5.51,
     "end": 12.459
    },
    {
     "text": "Rust's rule is simpler. After the move, s one is no longer valid, so it frees nothing.",
     "start": 13.279,
     "end": 19.591
    },
    {
     "text": "One owner. One free. And the compiler checks it before the program ever runs.",
     "start": 20.011,
     "end": 25.175
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.5,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 153,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "04": {
   "hash": "a5db1b52ac417adf",
   "takes": [
    "0cb7817875b7782311f9",
    "7a7873175922031ee451",
    "bdbfe6a65ac95187b6c0",
    "f56d05dbb3705a618387",
    "4702ab98ef18958d8e52"
   ],
   "duration": 21.966,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Let's measure it.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 0.887
    },
    {
     "text": "We move a vector of one million bytes into a function.",
     "start": 1.307,
     "end": 4.833
    },
    {
     "text": "The heap pointer? The same. Zero allocations. Only the twenty-four byte header was copied, to a new address.",
     "start": 5.253,
     "end": 12.033
    },
    {
     "text": "Now clone it. One allocation, a million bytes, and a brand new pointer.",
     "start": 12.753,
     "end": 17.404
    },
    {
     "text": "Rust never makes a deep copy... unless you write clone.",
     "start": 17.824,
     "end": 21.616
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.5,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 153,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "05": {
   "hash": "49f02f7c95821220",
   "takes": [
    "01d7bf6b970f20802ca7",
    "8943ae5d5559bf90e20c",
    "e44d9882dafe5ee6962d"
   ],
   "duration": 16.125,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Here's a subtle one.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 1.447
    },
    {
     "text": "After a move, the variable is empty. But is it empty forever?",
     "start": 1.867,
     "end": 7.183
    },
    {
     "text": "Line one makes it. Line two moves it. Line three assigns it again. What happens?",
     "start": 8.133,
     "end": 15.775
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.4,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 115,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "05a": {
   "hash": "8390f1c896d67cee",
   "takes": [
    "10b7dba3a24e8e014e5b",
    "18d2d3c8506377c3372f"
   ],
   "duration": 9.063,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "It compiles. Moving out leaves the variable empty, not cursed.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 5.608
    },
    {
     "text": "Assign a new value, and it's usable again.",
     "start": 6.028,
     "end": 8.713
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.4,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 119,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "06": {
   "hash": "38529db509a94623",
   "takes": [
    "9ea8c01738316224c594",
    "95ec31d6b92ccc03aad7",
    "d528ce36390d1dbe986a",
    "b7a1a2597dac482485fe",
    "091cd661f183de4b7859"
   ],
   "duration": 30.363,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "If you come from a language with move constructors, this next one is for you.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 5.782
    },
    {
     "text": "Standard move is only a cast. Your move constructor steals the buffer, and nulls out the source, because the source stays alive.",
     "start": 6.202,
     "end": 13.579
    },
    {
     "text": "So at the closing brace, that language runs two destructors. One of them on an empty shell.",
     "start": 14.299,
     "end": 20.395
    },
    {
     "text": "Rust runs one drop.",
     "start": 20.815,
     "end": 22.457
    },
    {
     "text": "A Rust move runs no code at all. The bytes are copied, and the compiler simply stops you using the source.",
     "start": 22.877,
     "end": 30.013
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.6,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 156,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "07": {
   "hash": "2e645772f499d13d",
   "takes": [
    "997710bdbec8512f6964",
    "2560f98297ab31333ce5",
    "d5b5a1531c31cf9306a9",
    "a35ed7a4b30c9123d2cf",
    "72ab4e692fa83a8b28fd"
   ],
   "duration": 24.162,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "One line of code. Six meanings.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 2.986
    },
    {
     "text": "In Python, Java and Go, both names stay usable, and both point at the same data.",
     "start": 3.406,
     "end": 10.286
    },
    {
     "text": "That language can copy it, or steal it and leave the source valid but unspecified.",
     "start": 10.706,
     "end": 15.439
    },
    {
     "text": "Rust copies the same few bytes that a shallow copy would, then ends the source.",
     "start": 16.159,
     "end": 21.044
    },
    {
     "text": "So there is still exactly one owner.",
     "start": 21.464,
     "end": 23.812
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.5,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 147,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "08": {
   "hash": "184031710b388ab6",
   "takes": [
    "60676a3571937eb4c71f",
    "f3c9c7b7299c0a8349a3",
    "e29224087f1502fa3490",
    "2c6eee5cee038080720e",
    "07eb92a56f0f2ff6aa41",
    "312e29c25e70c4a9ed5d"
   ],
   "duration": 32.906,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Some values are nothing but their bits.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 2.867
    },
    {
     "text": "An integer. A float. A boolean. A character. Arrays and tuples of those.",
     "start": 3.287,
     "end": 9.476
    },
    {
     "text": "Those types are Copy. Assigning one duplicates it, and the source stays valid.",
     "start": 9.896,
     "end": 15.028
    },
    {
     "text": "The test is simple: is the value just its bits? Does it own anything beyond them?",
     "start": 15.748,
     "end": 21.527
    },
    {
     "text": "A type can be Copy only if every field is. A shared reference is Copy. A mutable reference never is.",
     "start": 22.077,
     "end": 28.61
    },
    {
     "text": "And a type with a destructor... never.",
     "start": 29.03,
     "end": 32.556
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.7,
   "true_peak": -1.8,
   "wpm": 139,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "09": {
   "hash": "1b06f755a4dce137",
   "takes": [
    "611288a5cd9b5e6c7240",
    "28ff4134e7151c361fd0",
    "bc97c8620d20ba452ae5",
    "750d41f92ec709993602"
   ],
   "duration": 12.858,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Here's the most tempting mistake.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 1.965
    },
    {
     "text": "A wrapper around an integer handle... with a destructor that closes it.",
     "start": 2.385,
     "end": 6.276
    },
    {
     "text": "The integer is plain bits. So surely it can be Copy?",
     "start": 6.696,
     "end": 10.55
    },
    {
     "text": "Does this compile?",
     "start": 11.5,
     "end": 12.508
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.8,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 145,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "09a": {
   "hash": "5a0fc82c80bc00b8",
   "takes": [
    "b85a091c4d1e4996a012",
    "ceceaf5d5aaeeacf622f"
   ],
   "duration": 7.624,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "It doesn't compile. A type with a destructor can never implement Copy.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 4.506
    },
    {
     "text": "Two copies would mean two closes.",
     "start": 4.926,
     "end": 7.274
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.5,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 142,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "10": {
   "hash": "5a9bf34f231ad301",
   "takes": [
    "0028f15c504fe894cd69",
    "943b8124765fcb288531",
    "6aab25cd7c307352d376",
    "9c9918afeb4f69419907",
    "d8fc5c0afcc473c5e714"
   ],
   "duration": 22.054,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So where does the cost... actually show up?",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 2.901
    },
    {
     "text": "A copy allocates nothing. A move allocates nothing.",
     "start": 3.451,
     "end": 7.865
    },
    {
     "text": "Only the lines that clone light up.",
     "start": 8.285,
     "end": 10.795
    },
    {
     "text": "In Rust, if it's expensive, it says clone.",
     "start": 11.515,
     "end": 14.15
    },
    {
     "text": "Search a hot path for the clone method... and for similar methods that copy data... and you will find its deep copies.",
     "start": 14.57,
     "end": 21.704
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.4,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 144,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "11": {
   "hash": "e99129af02f45b25",
   "takes": [
    "22e0d6a8c038331f59a8",
    "10a90f2369af3a7ce2c5",
    "f1b2b323303fee8c2bc3"
   ],
   "duration": 12.268,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "People say moves are free. Are they?",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 3.409
    },
    {
     "text": "Here's a struct of four thousand and ninety-six bytes, moved into a function.",
     "start": 3.96,
     "end": 8.567
    },
    {
     "text": "How many bytes does the call copy?",
     "start": 9.387,
     "end": 11.918
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.4,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 132,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "11a": {
   "hash": "2090dd06d0c56c9e",
   "takes": [
    "e6d84cd12c462250517a",
    "51f2abbeb79b87d899c6"
   ],
   "duration": 13.643,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "A type of four thousand and ninety-six bytes. A move copies the whole value.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 5.023
    },
    {
     "text": "The optimizer sometimes removes the copy, but you can only count on a move being cheap when the value is small.",
     "start": 6.243,
     "end": 13.293
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.3,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 154,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "12": {
   "hash": "2a44ec46800006a7",
   "takes": [
    "8fa13fe56cade5d3f96c",
    "358f5744195db5dd6950",
    "a08375819f2ba3c45777",
    "e86065ae932aa7531284",
    "00bd32ecc16343ff47fd"
   ],
   "duration": 27.755,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So the cost of a move... depends on the size of the type.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 4.981
    },
    {
     "text": "A sixteen byte array moves sixteen bytes. A sixty-five thousand byte array can cost all of them.",
     "start": 5.401,
     "end": 12.372
    },
    {
     "text": "But a vector holding a million bytes? Twenty-four bytes. Just the header.",
     "start": 12.792,
     "end": 18.293
    },
    {
     "text": "Put it in a box, and the box is one pointer. Eight bytes... however big the contents.",
     "start": 18.713,
     "end": 25.591
    },
    {
     "text": "Tap a type, and see.",
     "start": 26.011,
     "end": 27.405
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.6,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 143,
