   "duration": 38.838,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "In one breath.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 1.366
    },
    {
     "text": "One owner, one drop.",
     "start": 1.786,
     "end": 4.195
    },
    {
     "text": "A move copies the bytes... and then ends the source.",
     "start": 4.615,
     "end": 7.814
    },
    {
     "text": "Copy means the bits are the whole value, and never with a destructor.",
     "start": 8.234,
     "end": 12.755
    },
    {
     "text": "Clone is the visible cost.",
     "start": 13.175,
     "end": 15.484
    },
    {
     "text": "A signature takes, borrows, borrows exclusively, or returns.",
     "start": 15.904,
     "end": 20.664
    },
    {
     "text": "For the use-after-move error, read why, where, and the later use. For the move-while-borrowed error, end the borrow first.",
     "start": 21.084,
     "end": 28.823
    },
    {
     "text": "One owner decides who destroys a value. Next: exactly when that happens. Drop order, temporaries, and why leaking memory is still safe.",
     "start": 29.543,
     "end": 38.488
    }
   ],
